"""This set's fixture generators, and the type of what they produce.

A generator is an ordinary module in this package, imported like any
other:

    from .fixtures import <name>

    expected = <name>.write(destination)

What is committed is the generator and its seed, never the fixture it
writes -- see PRAXIS.md, "What to commit".

**No generator is defined here, deliberately.** What one exposes is this
set's decision: how many there are, whether they are independent, and
whether the value a scorer checks is a single string at all. A stub here
would be a claim about all three, inherited by every set before anyone
knows whether it is true of this one.

'ExpectedType' is the one thing this package does declare, and it names
that contract rather than implementing it: the type of the value a
scorer checks a trial against. The scaffold defines it as 'type(None)'
-- nothing yet -- so a set that has not decided says so in the type
system instead of guessing at 'str'. Redefine it when the generator that
produces the value exists, and '__main__' and 'report' follow without
further edits.

Nothing is imported here. A generator is also runnable as
'python -m __PACKAGE__.fixtures.<name>', and importing one at package
level would make that execute it twice: once as a submodule of this
package, once as '__main__'.
"""

ExpectedType = type(None)
